Writing Desktop Applications in JavaScript<P>
<HR>
<H1>10  An organiser with sections</H1>
<HR>
Part Two builds a much bigger application: a personal organiser, with a diary, a list of things to do, an
address book, a notepad, a year planner and anniversaries, all kept in one file. In this chapter you'll plan
it, draw its ring binder, work out how its six sections share the binder, and bring the address book across
from !Contacts without changing a line of it. The program is <CODE>!Org10</CODE>.<P>

<A NAME=S1></A>
<H2>A binder on the screen</H2>
In the early 1990s, many people carried their lives in a ring binder: a Filofax, with a diary section, an
address section and pages for notes, each behind a coloured tab. In 1992 a small British company, Threadz,
turned that binder into a program. Lotus bought it and sold it as <I>Lotus Organizer</I>, and for most of the
1990s it was the best-selling organiser there was.<P>
Its window looked like a binder open on the desk: two pages side by side with the rings between them,
coloured section tabs down the right-hand edge, and turned-up corners at the bottom of the pages that you
clicked to turn over. Down the left was a toolbox, with a button for a new entry, today's date, and a
wastebasket you dragged entries into to throw them away. People liked it because it looked and behaved like
the paper binder they already knew: everything was always in the same place.<P>
!Organiser borrows those ideas, but it is a RISC OS application. The window's frame, its menus and its
dialogue boxes are the Wimp's, as the Style Guide asks; the binder is drawn inside the work area, where an
application may draw what it likes, just as Draw draws a drawing.<P>

<A NAME=S2></A>
<H2>Planning the organiser</H2>
As in chapter 1, start with what it remembers and what happens.<P>
<B>What it remembers.</B> Six kinds of entry, one for each section:<P>
<PRE>
Diary        appointments: a date, a time, what it is,
             perhaps an alarm, perhaps repeating
To do        things to do: a priority, dates, done or not
Address      contacts, exactly as !Contacts keeps them
Notepad      pages of notes, each with a title
Planner      coloured bars across days: holidays, projects
Anniversary  birthdays and other yearly days
</PRE>
and one more list, of <I>links</I> between entries (a meeting and the person you're meeting), which comes
in chapter 14. It all goes in one file: open your organiser and everything is there.<P>
<B>What happens.</B><P>
<PRE>
open a section by its tab; turn its pages
see the diary a week, a day, a month or a year at a time
add, change, move and delete entries
drag entries into the wastebasket
link entries together, and follow the links
be reminded of appointments; print pages
swap appointments with other programs
</PRE>
The chapters take those in turn. This one draws the binder, makes the sections, and does the whole of the
Address section, because we already have an address book.<P>

<A NAME=S3></A>
<H2>The binder</H2>
The binder is a toolkit module, <CODE>Binder</CODE>, because it only draws and measures: it doesn't know
what the sections are for. Its header comment says how it's used:<P>
<PRE>
// Binder - a ring binder drawn on a window's canvas: a leather cover,
// two pages open side by side with the rings between them, coloured
// section tabs down the right-hand edge, and turned-up corners at
// the bottom of the pages to turn them.
//
//   const binder = new Binder({ x, y, w, h, colour: '#6b3f22',
//     tabs: [{ name: 'Diary', colour: '#4f81bd' }, ...] });
//   binder.left, binder.right   the pages: { x, y, w, h }
//   binder.draw(g, current)     current: the open section's tab
//   binder.hit(x, y)            what's there: { tab: 2 },
//     { corner: -1 } (back) or { corner: 1 } (on), { page: 'left' },
//     { page: 'right' } or null
// It only draws and measures: what a click does is up to the program.
</PRE>
The constructor works out where everything goes, once, from the size it's given:<P>
<PRE>
const EDGE = 16, TAB = 84, RINGS = 34, CORNER = 30;
const PAPER = '#fffdf2', LINE = '#d8d2bc';

export class Binder {
  constructor({ x = 0, y = 0, w, h, tabs, colour = '#6b3f22' }) {
    Object.assign(this, { x, y, w, h, tabs, colour });
    const pw = Math.floor((w - 2 * EDGE - TAB - RINGS) / 2);
    const ph = h - 2 * EDGE;
    this.left = { x: x + EDGE, y: y + EDGE, w: pw, h: ph };
    this.right = { x: x + EDGE + pw + RINGS, y: y + EDGE, w: pw,
      h: ph };
    const th = Math.min(52, Math.floor((ph - 20) / tabs.length) - 4);
    this.tabRects = tabs.map((t, i) =&gt; ({
      x: this.right.x + pw - 2, y: this.right.y + 12 + i * (th + 4),
      w: TAB - 8, h: th }));
  }
</PRE>
From the outside in: a leather edge (<CODE>EDGE</CODE>) all round, a strip down the right for the tabs
(<CODE>TAB</CODE>), and the rings (<CODE>RINGS</CODE>) between the pages. What's left is shared equally by
the two pages, which are kept as rectangles, <CODE>left</CODE> and <CODE>right</CODE>. Every section draws
into those rectangles, so no section needs to know how big the window is. The tabs share the height of the
page between them (but aren't more than 52 pixels tall), so six fit - and so would seven.<P>
<CODE>draw</CODE> paints it in the order you would build it: the cover, the tabs that are behind the pages,
the pages, then the open section's tab in front of them (a little wider, so it seems to come out of the
binder), the rings and the corners:<P>
<PRE>
  draw(g, current) {
    const { x, y, w, h, left, right } = this;
    // the cover
    g.fillStyle = this.colour;
    g.fillRect(x, y, w, h);
    g.strokeStyle = 'rgba(0,0,0,0.35)';
    g.lineWidth = 2;
    g.strokeRect(x + 1, y + 1, w - 2, h - 2);
    // the tabs, behind the pages except the current one
    this.tabs.forEach((t, i) =&gt; {
      if (i !== current) this.tab(g, i);
    });
    for (const p of [left, right]) this.page(g, p);
    if (current != null) this.tab(g, current, true);
    this.rings(g);
    this.corner(g, left.x, left.y + left.h, -1);
    this.corner(g, right.x + right.w, right.y + right.h, 1);
  }
</PRE>
The rings are ellipses: <CODE>g.ellipse(x, y, rx, ry, rotation, start, end)</CODE> is the canvas's
oval-drawing cousin of <CODE>arc</CODE>, with two radii instead of one. Each ring is a grey oval with a
thinner, lighter one just above it for the shine.<P>
<P><CENTER><IMG SRC="PICS/O10Binder" WIDTH=910 HEIGHT=569></CENTER>
The other half of the module says what is at a point, so the program can tell a click on a tab from a click
on a page:<P>
<PRE>
  hit(x, y) {
    const inside = (r) =&gt; x &gt;= r.x &amp;&amp; x &lt; r.x + r.w &amp;&amp;
      y &gt;= r.y &amp;&amp; y &lt; r.y + r.h;
    const tab = this.tabRects.findIndex(inside);
    if (tab &gt;= 0) return { tab };
    const { left, right } = this;
    if (inside(left)) {
      if (x - left.x + (left.y + left.h - y) &lt; CORNER) {
        return { corner: -1 };
      }
      return { page: 'left' };
    }
    if (inside(right)) {
      if (right.x + right.w - x + (right.y + right.h - y) &lt; CORNER) {
        return { corner: 1 };
      }
      return { page: 'right' };
    }
    return null;
  }
</PRE>
<CODE>findIndex</CODE> tries each tab's rectangle. A page corner is a triangle, not a rectangle: a point is
inside the bottom-left triangle when its distance in from the left edge plus its distance up from the
bottom is less than the corner's size. <CODE>hit</CODE> returns a small object describing what's there -
<CODE>{ tab: 2 }</CODE>, <CODE>{ corner: -1 }</CODE> - and deciding what that means is left to the
program.<P>

<A NAME=S4></A>
<H2>Sections</H2>
The six sections are different, but the window does the same things with each of them: it asks the open
one to draw its pages, and passes it the clicks and keys the binder doesn't use. That is the
<I>section interface</I>, and <CODE>Paper</CODE> writes it down, as a base class every section extends:<P>
<PRE>
// Paper - what every section of the organiser has in common: the
// Section class it is made from, and help for drawing on the pages.
//
// A section is shown on the binder's two pages. The window (./Organi-
// serWindow) asks it to (the ones marked with a chapter come later:
// this stage's sections don't need them yet):
//   draw(g, left, right)      draw both pages (rectangles {x,y,w,h})
//   click(ev), double(ev)     the mouse, in the work area
//   drag(ev)                  a drag started (12: moving an entry)
//   key(ev)                   a key (true if it used it)
//   turn(dir)                 the page corners: -1 back, 1 on
//   today()                   the Today button (11)
//   newEntry()                a new entry (the Insert key)
//   views, view, setView(v)   the view buttons (11: Week, Month ...)
//   go(ref)                   show that entry
//   describe(ref)             a line about an entry (12)
//   remove(ref)               delete an entry (12)
//   dropped(ref, x, y)        an entry dragged to x, y (12)
//   show() / hide()           it is being shown or put away
// While drawing, a section records where its entries are with
// box(x, y, w, h, ref), so the window can tell what is clicked.
export class Section {
  constructor(org, { id, name, colour, views = [], newHelp }) {
    Object.assign(this, { org, id, name, colour, views, newHelp });
    this.view = views[0] ?? '';
    this.boxes = [];
  }
  get doc() { return this.org.doc; }

  /** Remember an entry drawn at x, y, w, h, so clicks can find it. */
  box(x, y, w, h, ref) {
    ref = { section: this.id, ...ref };
    this.boxes.push({ x, y, w, h, ref });
  }
  /** The entry drawn at x, y (the last one drawn wins). */
  at(x, y) {
    return this.boxes.findLast((b) =&gt; inside(b, x, y)) ?? null;
  }
  setView(v) { this.view = v; this.org.redraw(); }

  // what a section doesn't do, it needn't write
  draw() {}
  click() { return false; }
  double() { return false; }
  drag() { return false; }
  key() { return false; }
  turn() {}
  today() {}
  newEntry() {}
  go() {}
  describe(ref) { return `${this.name} entry`; }
  remove() {}
  dropped() {}
  show() {}
  hide() {}
}
</PRE>
The list in the comment is every question the window may ask a section; the ones marked with a chapter
number are asked from that chapter on, and this stage's sections don't need them yet. The class answers all
of them by doing nothing: <CODE>draw() {}</CODE>, <CODE>click() { return false; }</CODE> and so on. So a section only
writes the methods it needs, and the window can call any of them on any section without checking first.
You'll see the list fill up over the next few chapters, as sections learn to add, move and delete entries.<P>
The one thing every section does do is remember where it drew its entries. While drawing, it calls
<CODE>box(x, y, w, h, ref)</CODE> for each one, where <CODE>ref</CODE> is a small object saying which entry
it is; afterwards, <CODE>at(x, y)</CODE> finds the entry at a point. <CODE>findLast</CODE> is
<CODE>find</CODE> starting from the end: the entry drawn last is on top, so it's the one you clicked.
The ref gets the section's <CODE>id</CODE> added to it, so it says where the entry lives as well as which one
it is: <CODE>{ section: 'address', id: 7 }</CODE>. That will matter when entries are dragged between
sections and linked.<P>
<CODE>Paper</CODE> also has some drawing helpers every section uses: <CODE>heading</CODE> for a page's
title with a line in the section's colour, <CODE>text</CODE> for text cut off at a width, and
<CODE>ruled</CODE> for lines across a page.<P>

<H3>Sections still to be written</H3>
In this chapter only the Address section is real, but all six tabs should be there. The others are made by
a function that makes a class:<P>
<PRE>
/** A section still to be written: just its name on the page. */
const blank = (id, name, colour) =&gt; class extends Section {
  constructor(org) { super(org, { id, name, colour }); }
  draw(g, L) { heading(g, L, name, colour); }
};
</PRE>
<CODE>blank</CODE> is an arrow function that returns a <I>class expression</I>: <CODE>class extends Section
{ ... }</CODE> without a name, written where a value is expected, just as a function can be. Classes are
values in JavaScript - you can pass them around, keep them in arrays and return them. Each call of
<CODE>blank</CODE> makes a new class; its constructor and <CODE>draw</CODE> can use <CODE>id</CODE>,
<CODE>name</CODE> and <CODE>colour</CODE> because they are inside <CODE>blank</CODE>, whose parameters they
are. The window makes its sections from a list of classes:<P>
<PRE>
    this.sections = [blank('diary', 'Diary', '#6fa8dc'),
      blank('todo', 'To do', '#e06666'),
      AddressPage,
      blank('notes', 'Notepad', '#f6b26b'),
      blank('planner', 'Planner', '#b4a7d6'),
      blank('anniversary', 'Anniversary', '#e377c2'),
    ].map((S) =&gt; new S(this));
</PRE>
<CODE>map((S) =&gt; new S(this))</CODE> makes one object of each class, passing the window. In each later
chapter a line of that list changes: <CODE>blank('diary', 'Diary', '#6fa8dc')</CODE> becomes
<CODE>DiaryPage</CODE>, and so on, until there are no blanks left.<P>

<A NAME=S5></A>
<H2>The window</H2>
The window is 900 by 540 pixels, with the binder filling its work area:<P>
<PRE>
export class OrganiserWindow {
  constructor(doc) {
    this.doc = doc;
    this.app = doc.app;
    const n = this.app.docs.size % 5;
    const w = this.win = this.app.task.createWindow({
      title: doc.title, x: 40 + 24 * n, y: 40 + 24 * n, w: W, h: H,
      extent: { w: W, h: H }, colours: { workBg: 1 },
      workButton: 'clickdragdouble',
      flags: { back: true, close: true, title: true, moveable: true },
      help: 'This is your organiser.|MClick a tab to open a ' +
        'section; click a page corner to turn the page.',
    });
    this.sections = [blank('diary', 'Diary', '#6fa8dc'),
      blank('todo', 'To do', '#e06666'),
      AddressPage,
      blank('notes', 'Notepad', '#f6b26b'),
      blank('planner', 'Planner', '#b4a7d6'),
      blank('anniversary', 'Anniversary', '#e377c2'),
    ].map((S) =&gt; new S(this));
    this.current = 0;
    this.binder = new Binder({ x: SIDE, y: 0, w: W - SIDE, h: H,
      colour: this.app.prefs.colour,
      tabs: this.sections.map((s) =&gt; ({ name: s.name,
        colour: s.colour })) });
    w.useCanvas((g) =&gt; this.draw(g));
    w.on('click', (ev) =&gt; this.click(ev));
    w.on('doubleclick', (ev) =&gt; this.section.double(ev));
    w.on('key', (ev) =&gt; this.key(ev));
    w.on('close', (ev) =&gt; { ev.preventDefault(); this.doc.close(); });
    this.offs = doc.parts.map((p) =&gt;
      p.on('change', () =&gt; this.redraw()));
    w.open({ behind: 'top' });
    this.section.show();
    this.refresh();
  }
</PRE>
<CODE>workButton: 'clickdragdouble'</CODE> reports clicks, drags and double-clicks - the diary will need all
three. The window listens to every part of the document (<CODE>doc.parts</CODE>), so whatever changes,
the binder is redrawn: the same one-source-of-truth idea as chapter 4, with seven models instead of one.<P>
Drawing is the binder, then the open section on its two pages:<P>
<PRE>
  // ------------------------------------------------ drawing
  draw(g) {
    this.binder.colour = this.app.prefs.colour;
    this.binder.draw(g, this.current);
    const s = this.section;
    s.boxes = [];
    s.draw(g, ...this.pages);
  }
</PRE>
<CODE>...this.pages</CODE> spreads the array of two pages into two arguments, so
<CODE>s.draw(g, ...this.pages)</CODE> is <CODE>s.draw(g, left, right)</CODE>. Emptying
<CODE>s.boxes</CODE> first means the entries recorded are the ones on the screen now.<P>
A click goes to the binder first, then to the section:<P>
<PRE>
  // ------------------------------------------------ the mouse
  click(ev) {
    if (ev.button === 'menu') { this.openMenu(ev); return true; }
    this.focus();
    const hit = this.binder.hit(ev.x, ev.y);
    if (hit?.tab != null) { this.open(hit.tab); return true; }
    if (hit?.corner) { this.section.turn(hit.corner); this.refresh();
      return true; }
    this.section.click(ev);
    return true;
  }
</PRE>
and keys go to the section first - so the Address section can take typed letters - then to the window,
which turns pages with Page Up and Page Down, saves with F3, and opens sections with Ctrl-1 to Ctrl-6:<P>
<PRE>
  // ------------------------------------------------ the keyboard
  key(ev) {
    if (this.section.key(ev)) return true;
    switch (ev.code) {
      case 0x183: this.doc.quickSave(); return true;         // F3
      case 0x19F: this.section.turn(-1); break;          // Page Up
      case 0x19E: this.section.turn(1); break;           // Page Down
      case 0x1CD: this.section.newEntry(); return true;      // Insert
      default:                             // Ctrl-1 ... Ctrl-6: tabs
        if (ev.ctrl &amp;&amp; ev.code &gt;= 49 &amp;&amp; ev.code &lt;= 54) {
          this.open(ev.code - 49);
          return true;
        }
        return undefined;
    }
    this.refresh();
    return true;
  }
</PRE>
A key event says whether Ctrl was held down (<CODE>ev.ctrl</CODE>); the digits 1 to 6 are character codes
49 to 54, so <CODE>ev.code - 49</CODE> is the section's number. The menu lists the sections too, ticked
and with those keys beside them.<P>

<A NAME=S6></A>
<H2>The address section: !Contacts again</H2>
The Address section is a whole address book, and we have one: !Contacts' <CODE>Book</CODE> class (chapters
1 to 9) keeps contacts, sorts and searches them, and turns them into JSON; its <CODE>editCard</CODE>
function (chapter 3) is a dialogue box for one contact. The organiser uses both exactly as they are.<P>
They are copied into <CODE>!Org10</CODE> as the files <CODE>Model</CODE> and <CODE>Card</CODE>. The book's
examples keep one copy of each, in !Contacts: <CODE>!Org10</CODE> has a file <CODE>Reuse.list</CODE> naming
the files it borrows, and when the examples are put on the disc each one is copied in, just as
<CODE>Toolkit.list</CODE> copies the toolkit:<P>
<PRE>
# !Contacts' address book, used unchanged for the Address section
!Contacts/Model
!Contacts/Card
</PRE>
In your own programs, copy the files yourself, and copy them again if you improve the original. This works
because <CODE>Book</CODE> and <CODE>editCard</CODE> were written to know nothing of Contacts' windows: the
model/view split of chapter 1 pays off. !Contacts' <CODE>MainWindow</CODE> couldn't be reused - it is a
window, and the organiser shows contacts differently - but the part that remembers contacts, and the card
that edits one, move across untouched.<P>
The section itself, <CODE>AddressPage</CODE>, is the organiser's view of the address book, on binder
paper: the names under one letter on the left page, the chosen person's details on the right, and a tab
for each letter down the right-hand edge - starting below the page's heading and stopping above the
turned-up corner, so neither covers the other.<P>
<PRE>
export class AddressPage extends Section {
  constructor(org) {
    super(org, { id: 'address', name: 'Address', colour: '#93c47d',
      newHelp: 'add a person to the address book' });
    this.letter = 'A';
    this.chosen = null;                   // the id of the card shown
    this.tabs = [];
  }
  names() {
    return this.doc.book.list({ sort: 'last' })
      .filter((c) =&gt; initial(c) === this.letter);
  }

  draw(g, L, R) {
    heading(g, L, 'Address', this.colour, this.letter);
    ruled(g, L, L.y + TOP + ROW, ROW);
    const names = this.names();
    names.forEach((c, i) =&gt; {
      const y = L.y + TOP + i * ROW;
      if (y + ROW &gt; L.y + L.h - 10) return;
      if (c.id === this.chosen) {
        g.fillStyle = '#e4f0de';
        g.fillRect(L.x + 10, y, L.w - 20, ROW);
      }
      text(g, nameOf(c), L.x + 14, y + ROW / 2, L.w - 140,
        'bold 13px Homerton');
      text(g, phoneOf(c), L.x + L.w - 124, y + ROW / 2, 112);
      this.box(L.x + 10, y, L.w - 20, ROW, { id: c.id });
    });
    if (!names.length) {
      text(g, `No names under ${this.letter}.`, L.x + 14,
        L.y + TOP + 12, L.w - 28, 'italic 12px Homerton', FAINT);
    }
    this.card(g, R);
    this.letterTabs(g, R);
  }
</PRE>
<CODE>names</CODE> asks the book for its contacts sorted by surname - <CODE>list({ sort: 'last' })</CODE>,
from chapter 6 - and keeps those whose name starts with the chosen letter. Each name is recorded with
<CODE>box</CODE>, so clicking it chooses it:<P>
<PRE>
  click(ev) {
    const t = this.tabs.find((b) =&gt; inside(b, ev.x, ev.y));
    if (t) { this.turnTo(t.l); return true; }
    const box = this.at(ev.x, ev.y);
    if (box) { this.chosen = box.ref.id; this.org.redraw(); }
    return true;
  }
  turnTo(l) {
    this.letter = l;
    this.chosen = this.names()[0]?.id ?? null;
    this.org.redraw();
  }
  async double(ev) {
    const box = this.at(ev.x, ev.y);
    if (box) await this.edit(box.ref.id);
  }
  key(ev) {                          // type a letter to turn to it
    const l = ev.char?.toUpperCase();
    if (!l || !LETTERS.includes(l) || ev.ctrl) return false;
    this.turnTo(l);
    return true;
  }
  turn(dir) {
    const i = LETTERS.indexOf(this.letter) + dir;
    this.turnTo(LETTERS[(i + 26) % 26]);
  }
  today() {}

  async edit(id) {
    const c = this.doc.book.get(id);
    const v = await editCard(this.org.app.task, c, nameOf(c));
    this.org.focus();
    if (v) this.doc.book.put({ ...c, ...v });
  }
  async newEntry() {
    const v = await editCard(this.org.app.task, {}, 'New contact');
    this.org.focus();
    if (!v) return;
    const c = this.doc.book.add(v);
    this.go({ id: c.id });
    this.org.redraw();
  }
  go(ref) {
    const c = this.doc.book.get(ref.id);
    if (!c) return;
    this.letter = initial(c);
    this.chosen = c.id;
  }
</PRE>
Typing a letter turns to it, the page corners go to the letter before or after, and a double-click opens
!Contacts' card to change the contact. When the card closes, <CODE>book.put</CODE> keeps the changed
contact, the book emits <CODE>change</CODE>, and the window redraws.<P>
