<A NAME=S7></A>
<H2>One file for everything</H2>
The organiser keeps everything in one file of its own type, &amp;1C5, named <I>Organisr</I> (a file type's
name can only be eight letters long). Its <CODE>!Boot</CODE> is !Contacts' with a different name and number:<P>
<PRE>
| !Boot file for !Org10: the Filer runs it when it first shows the
| application, so the icons and the file type are known.
Set Org10$Dir &lt;Obey$Dir&gt;
IconSprites &lt;Obey$Dir&gt;.!Sprites
Set File$Type_1C5 Organisr
| double-clicking an Organiser file runs the Organiser with it (%% is
| a %: Obey would put its own parameters in place of a plain %*0)
Set Alias$@RunType_1C5 Run &lt;Org10$Dir&gt;.!Run %%*0
</PRE>
The other sections will each keep a list of entries, and they all need the same things: add, change, remove,
look up by id, save as JSON, and tell listeners when something changes. So there is one class for all of
them, <CODE>Collection</CODE>, in the module <CODE>Store</CODE>:<P>
<PRE>
// Store - what the organiser remembers, section by section, with no
// windows in it. Each kind of entry is kept in a Collection: a Map
// from id to entry, which emits 'change' whenever it changes.
//
//   const diary = new Collection(data.diary);
//   diary.add({ date, text })
//   diary.update(id, fields); diary.put(entry); diary.remove(id)
//   diary.get(id), diary.all(), diary.toJSON()
import { Emitter } from './Toolkit/Emitter';

export class Collection extends Emitter {
  constructor(items = []) {
    super();
    this.items = new Map();            // id -&gt; entry
    this.nextId = 1;
    for (const x of items) this.put(x, false);
  }
  get size() { return this.items.size; }
  get(id) { return this.items.get(id); }
  all() { return [...this.items.values()]; }
  add(fields) {
    const x = { ...fields, id: fields.id ?? this.nextId };
    this.put(x);
    return x;
  }
  update(id, fields) {
    const x = { ...this.items.get(id), ...fields, id };
    this.put(x);
    return x;
  }
  /** Keep exactly this entry (tell listeners unless quiet). */
  put(x, tell = true) {
    this.items.set(x.id, x);
    this.nextId = Math.max(this.nextId, x.id + 1);
    if (tell) this.emit('change', x);
  }
  remove(id) {
    const x = this.items.get(id);
    this.items.delete(id);
    this.emit('change', x);
    return x;
  }
  toJSON() { return this.all(); }
}
</PRE>
It is !Contacts' <CODE>Book</CODE> without anything about contacts: a <CODE>Map</CODE> from id to entry,
<CODE>nextId</CODE> for new ones, and a <CODE>change</CODE> event. <CODE>toJSON</CODE> gives an array of
the entries, so a Collection can be handed straight to <CODE>JSON.stringify</CODE>.<P>
The <CODE>Document</CODE> is the one from chapter 7, grown: instead of one Book, it has a Collection for each
section and !Contacts' Book for the addresses:<P>
<PRE>
export class Document {
  constructor(app, data = {}, filename = '') {
    this.app = app;
    this.filename = filename;          // '' until it's saved
    this.modified = false;
    // every section's entries (kept even while its page is blank)
    this.diary = new Collection(data.diary);
    this.todo = new Collection(data.todo);
    this.book = Book.fromJSON({ format: 'Contacts',
      contacts: data.contacts ?? [] });
    this.notes = new Collection(data.notes);
    this.planner = new Collection(data.planner);
    this.anniversaries = new Collection(data.anniversaries);
    this.links = new Collection(data.links);
    for (const part of this.parts) {
      part.on('change', () =&gt; this.setModified(true));
    }
    this.view = new OrganiserWindow(this);
    app.docs.add(this);
  }
  get parts() {
    return [this.diary, this.todo, this.book, this.notes,
      this.planner, this.anniversaries, this.links];
  }

  static async load(app, path) {
    const data = JSON.parse(await vfs.readText(path));
    if (data?.format !== 'Organiser') {
      throw new Error('This is not an Organiser file');
    }
    return new Document(app, data, path);
  }
</PRE>
<CODE>parts</CODE> lists them all, so the document can listen to every one (any change sets the
<CODE>*</CODE> in the title) and so can the window. Saving writes them all into one object:<P>
<PRE>
  // ------------------------------------------------ saving
  get data() {
    return JSON.stringify({
      format: 'Organiser', version: 1,
      diary: this.diary, todo: this.todo,
      contacts: this.book.all(), notes: this.notes,
      planner: this.planner, anniversaries: this.anniversaries,
      links: this.links,
    }, null, 1) + '\n';
  }
  save(path) {
    vfs.writeFile(path, this.data, { filetype: this.app.TYPE });
    this.filename = vfs.canonical(path);
    this.setModified(false);
  }
</PRE>
The contacts are saved as a plain list, just as !Contacts would save them, with <CODE>format</CODE> and
<CODE>version</CODE> at the top as in chapter 7. As the comment in the constructor says, the document keeps
every section's entries even while this stage shows their pages blank. It still reads and writes them, so if
you open an organiser file made by a later stage, change an address and save it, the diary and to-do list in
it aren't lost.<P>
Closing, the Save box and the Discard/Cancel/Save question are chapter 7's, and <CODE>!RunImage</CODE> is
!Contacts': one icon on the icon bar, one running copy, and a <CODE>Document</CODE> for each open file.<P>
As always, the part without windows is tested by <CODE>Check</CODE>:<P>
<PRE>
export default function start() {
  const c = new Collection([{ id: 4, text: 'kept' }]);
  let told = 0;
  c.on('change', () =&gt; told++);
  const x = c.add({ text: 'new' });
  check('a new entry gets the next id', x.id, 5);
  c.update(4, { done: true });
  check('update keeps the other fields', c.get(4),
    { id: 4, text: 'kept', done: true });
  c.remove(5);
  check('removed', [c.size, c.get(5)], [1, undefined]);
  check('listeners told of each change', told, 3);
  check('saved as a list', JSON.stringify(c), '[{&quot;id&quot;:4,' +
    '&quot;text&quot;:&quot;kept&quot;,&quot;done&quot;:true}]');
  print(failed ? `${failed} FAILED` : 'all ok');
}
</PRE>
